# Fix Frozen Login & 401 Interceptors

## The Issue 📋
When a user's session expires in the background (15m JWT timeout), the frontend continues to use the dead JWT. Because many files import `axios` directly (bypassing the [useApi](file:///c:/Users/vaibh/Documents/Ayur-stock%20pro/hooks/index.ts#5-42) hook), `401 Unauthorized` errors aren't caught, causing components to stall in infinite loading states. Furthermore, attempting to log in again fails because old dead tokens continuously clutter the client state.

## Proposed Changes

### 1. Fix [app/login/page.tsx](file:///c:/Users/vaibh/Documents/Ayur-stock%20pro/app/login/page.tsx)
- **Pre-flight wipe:** Before `axios.post('/api/auth/login')`, we will aggressively call:
  ```typescript
  localStorage.removeItem('token');
  localStorage.removeItem('user');
  await axios.post('/api/auth/logout').catch(() => {});
  ```
- This ensures an absolutely cleanly reset state for incoming new credentials without old tokens causing auth mismatches.

### 2. Create Global Interceptor `lib/axios.ts`
- Create a new file `lib/axios.ts` that safely mutates the global Axios singleton on the client:
  ```typescript
  import axios from 'axios';
  // Intercept all 401 errors application-wide
  axios.interceptors.response.use(
    (res) => res,
    (err) => {
      if (err.response?.status === 401) {
        localStorage.removeItem('token');
        localStorage.removeItem('user');
        window.location.href = '/login';
      }
      return Promise.reject(err);
    }
  );
  ```

### 3. Attach Interceptor Globally
- Import `import '@/lib/axios';` directly into [app/providers.tsx](file:///c:/Users/vaibh/Documents/Ayur-stock%20pro/app/providers.tsx) (which wraps [app/layout.tsx](file:///c:/Users/vaibh/Documents/Ayur-stock%20pro/app/layout.tsx)).
- This guarantees the singleton behavior is activated the exact millisecond the React app boots, automatically catching and blocking any rogue `401`s from any of the 18 direct Axios imports scattered across the application.
